arc:marc_nuls/NULS_PHC_180925.mrc:96777759:1948","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_044.bib.mrc:142893205:3728"],"title":"Are we smart enough to know how smart animals are?","notes":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 291-318) and index.","identifiers":{"goodreads":["26530322"]},"edition_name":"1st ed.","local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223114772701","urn:sfpl:31223114772743","urn:sfpl:31223114772644","urn:sfpl:31223114772693","urn:sfpl:31223114772669","urn:sfpl:31223114772792","urn:sfpl:31223117195157","urn:sfpl:31223117195132","urn:sfpl:31223114211304","urn:sfpl:31223114211312","urn:sfpl:31223114772750","urn:sfpl:31223114772784","urn:sfpl:31223114772677","urn:sfpl:31223114211338","urn:sfpl:31223114211296","urn:sfpl:31223114772768","urn:sfpl:31223114772685","urn:sfpl:31223114211288","urn:sfpl:31223114772651","urn:sfpl:31223114772776","urn:bwbsku:T2-ABE-762"],"publish_date":"2016","by_statement":"Frans de Waal ; with drawings by the author.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL21299028W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"covers":[12514840],"ocaid":"arewesmartenough0000waal_s1a8","isbn_13":["9780393246186"],"lccn":["2015049994"],"oclc_numbers":["921868861"],"dewey_decimal_class":["591.5/13"],"lc_classifications":["QL785 .W127 2016","QL785.W127 2016"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"What separates your mind from that of an animal? Is it the ability to design tools; a sense of self; or the grasp of past and future? In recent decades these claims have eroded, or even been disproven outright, by a revolution in the study of animal cognition. Waal explores both the scope and the depth of animal intelligence, offering a firsthand account of how science has stood traditional behaviorism on its head by revealing how smart animals really are, and how we've underestimated their abilities for too long."},"latest_revision":13,"revision":13,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2017-08-05T03:51:33.251058"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-07-31T20:01:07.501418"}},{"works":[{"key":"/works/OL21299028W"}],"title":"Zijn we slim genoeg om te weten hoe slim dieren zijn?","publishers":["Atlas Contact"],"publish_date":"April 12, 2016","key":"/books/OL60324802M","type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"identifiers":{},"covers":[15128161],"isbn_13":["9789045028590"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/dut"}],"translation_of":"Are We Smart Enough to Know How Smart Animals Are?","translated_from":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Kan een octopus gereedschap gebruiken? Weten chimpansees wat eerlijk is? Kan een vogel raden wat een andere vogel weet? Voelen ratten empathie met hun vrienden? Niet zo lang geleden zou het antwoord op al deze vragen ‘Nee’ geweest zijn, maar nu zijn we er niet meer zo zeker van. Als je goed kijkt, blijken dieren een stuk slimmer te zijn dan we dachten.\r\nWat is het verschil tussen menselijke en dierlijke intelligentie, en hoe komen we daarachter? Het zijn vragen die Frans de Waal al zijn hele carrière bezighouden. De laatste jaren heeft het onderzoek naar dierlijke intelligentie een grote vlucht genomen. Onderzoekers proberen zich steeds meer te verplaatsen in het standpunt van de dieren. In Zijn we slim genoeg om te weten hoe slim dieren zijn? maakt Frans de Waal de balans op. Een fascinerend boek dat je op een werkelijk andere manier laat kijken naar wat dier én mens kunnen."},"physical_format":"Ebook","number_of_pages":463,"latest_revision":3,"revision":3,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-10-01T17:28:29.473234"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-10-01T17:31:05.286841"}},{"publishers":["Norton & Company, Incorporated, W. W."],"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2020-08-30T11:49:58.634699"},"source_records":["bwb:9780393246193"],"title":"Are We Smart Enough to Know How Smart Animals Are?","number_of_pages":352,"isbn_13":["9780393246193"],"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2020-08-30T11:49:58.634699"},"langua